      "before": "**Part A – velocity as an arrow:** A ball rolls horizontally off a table edge at 2.0 m/s and hits the floor 0.30 s later. Air resistance is negligible; use g = 10 m/s².\n1. For t = 0.15 s and for the moment of impact t = 0.30 s, determine the horizontal and the vertical velocity component. Explain why the horizontal component stays the same. (4 marks)\n2. For both instants, draw the components to scale as arrows, for example 1 cm ≙ 0.5 m/s, and construct the velocity arrow from them. State your scale. (4 marks)\n3. Calculate the magnitudes of the two velocities. (3 marks)\n4. Measure in your drawing the angle to the horizontal at which the ball hits the floor. Explain why this angle increases during the flight. (2 marks)\n\n**Part B – two frames of reference:** A flat toy cart moves on the floor at a constant 2.0 m/s in the same direction as the ball. At the moment the ball leaves the table, the cart is exactly below the table edge; its height is negligible.\n5. Describe the motion of the ball as an observer on the cart sees it, and state its velocity relative to the cart at impact. Show with an arrow addition that, together with the velocity of the cart, this gives the impact velocity from part A again. (3 marks)",
